      "urpx:forRatePlanModifier": {
        "@id": "pge:e-rsmart"
      },
      "urpx:hasCoverageClaim": {
        "@id": "pge:cc-e-rsmart-2025-10-31",
        "@type": "urpx:CoverageClaim",
        "urpx:completenessResult": {
          "@id": "urpx:partial"
        },
        "urpx:selfContained": true,
        "urpx:explainedBy": {
          "@type": "urpx:Evaluation",
          "@id": "pge:cc-e-rsmart-2025-10-31-evaluation",
          "urpx:evaluationKind": {
            "@id": "urpx:coverageEvaluation"
          },
          "urpx:description": "Two provisions of the published Schedule E-RSMART are not represented in this filing at all, and a third is carried only as prose. The Sheet 5 Bill Protection transitional incentive, under which a new participant pays no more than it would have under its otherwise applicable schedule through its first full Summer Season, which for this purpose runs May 1 through October 31 rather than the June 1 through September 30 summer of the underlying residential schedules, computed cumulatively at the end of the season and credited on the next regular bill. The Sheet 2 bill-period multiplier on both credits: the SmartRate Non-High-Price Period Credit of $0.00636 per kWh and the Participation Credit of $0.00167 per kWh are each multiplied by the number of SmartDays falling in the bill period, and neither applies at all in a bill period containing no SmartDay. This filing carries the two per-event rates as its unit prices, and states the multiplier and the at-least-one-SmartDay condition only as prose on each credit's applicability rule, whose own machine-readable condition is an opaque yes-or-no predicate the consumer must evaluate; the SmartDay count itself appears nowhere as data. The Sheet 5 grandfathering for customers dual enrolled in SmartRate and SmartAC before October 26, 2018, who may remain in both programs but receive no demand response energy payment during the SmartDay High-Price Period. Two further items are scope decisions rather than gaps. The Sheet 4 activation trigger (an average day-ahead maximum temperature forecast for San Jose, Concord, Red Bluff, Sacramento and Fresno of 98 degrees Fahrenheit on non-holiday weekdays and 105 degrees on holidays and weekends, with CAISO alerts, high forecast spot prices and testing as further grounds, and PG&E retaining sole discretion not to call an event) settles which days are SmartDays rather than what a SmartDay costs, and is carried as prose on the price update rule together with the 4:00 to 9:00 p.m. High-Price Period, which is the same window on all five base schedules even though their own peak periods differ. The Sheet 1 enrollment gates (the SmartMeter requirement, the five-business-day lag before the next billing cycle, and the exclusions for master-metered, net energy metering, standby, direct access, community choice aggregation and Rule 22.1 transitional bundled service customers) are enrollment operations, so the filing carries a single eligibility condition: that the customer has elected SmartRate.",
          "urpx:evaluatedOn": "2026-09-21T00:00:00"
        }
      }
    }
  }
